cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or build on these experiments, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "ITASORL: Incidental Detection of Surrogate Worlds by a From-Scratch Digital Organism"
authors:
  - family-names: "Tate"
    given-names: "Levy"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0009-1337-0709"
repository-code: "https://github.com/iLevyTate/ItaSoRL"
license: MIT
version: 1.4.0
date-released: "2026-10-15"
abstract: >-
  ITASORL measures whether a recurrent agent trained from scratch, with no
  imported semantic knowledge and no reward for detection, comes to carry in its
  state whether its world runs on authentic base dynamics or a substrate-flawed
  surrogate, set against how detectable the flaw is to a privileged detector at a
  stated detector-side noise. Hand-authored flaws (L1 discretization, L2 rollout
  drift) are detectable but did not meet the registered encoding criterion; at
  the L3 gate's detector noise the L2 flaws read 0.62 to 0.65, so the matched
  contrast is L1 against L3. Against a learned-dynamics fingerprint (L3),
  survival-trained agents carrying a next-observation auxiliary at the
  registered 300-update budget, with the trainer corrected in October 2026,
  read 0.733 against 0.589 for a prediction-only arm. The registered rule is
  not met, because the L0 gate is open on the registered evaluation-world pair
  (0.559); the decodability clauses are met. Across eight independent pairs the
  gate is equivalent to chance on the pooled test, while it accepts on two of
  the eight draws individually. Without the auxiliary the arm reads 0.613 at 300 updates
  and 0.685 at 450, so the auxiliary result is bounded by budget. At matched
  evaluation input a prediction-trained trunk reads the world within about
  0.01 of the survival trunk (0.721 against 0.733): the signal rides on the trajectories the foraging policy
  produces and is kept by a trunk shaped by next-observation prediction. With
  death switched off and a pellet-directed reward in place of survival, the
  agent's state reads 0.796 and the registered rule is met with every gate, so
  survival is not required (FINDINGS section 18). A
  hand-authored, temporally coherent drag of matched one-step size is read
  through the same direction while white jitter is not, so nothing here is
  specific to learned dynamics. A common-garden control passes its frozen rule
  on the historical agents (0.666, 0.684): prefix condition
  remains decodable after restoring authentic dynamics, and on the corrected agents that is
  carried mostly by the physical footprint of the prefix, not by memory (see
  FINDINGS section 10.6.1 and 17.7). Four readouts on the saved agents
  (FINDINGS section 23) find the world direction faint in the state's own
  geometry, nothing of the agent's own world kept beyond the senses, no change
  in behavior when the decoded direction is removed while the agent acts, and
  a stored component that grows and reorganizes across training snapshots.
  Corrections and what each does not establish: docs/CORRECTIONS.md.
keywords:
  - artificial-life
  - reinforcement-learning
  - self-supervised-learning
  - simulation-hypothesis
  - world-models
